# FINAL EXPECTED RESULT

The game should now support a much faster and more versatile combat experience.

The player can control traditional spider-legged combat mechs, rapidly dash across the battlefield, grapple onto infrastructure, swing between buildings, and launch themselves into combat.

A new rocket-powered flying mech adds an entirely new way to play.

Enemy forces should also contain flying machines that can pursue, flank, and attack from the air.

The procedural leg animations must remain smooth at high speeds.

The camera shake should be completely configurable.

The dash should have stylish afterimages and satisfying acceleration.

The grappling system should make the battlefield feel more interactive and vertical.

The flight system should make aerial combat a fully supported part of the game.

**MOST IMPORTANT DESIGN DIRECTION:**

I want the game to feel like a combination of heavy futuristic mech warfare and extremely dynamic movement-based combat.

One moment, the player should be firing a massive artillery cannon.

The next moment, they should be dashing between enemy attacks, grappling onto a building, swinging around it, and launching themselves back into combat.

Above them, rocket-powered enemy machines should be chasing allied units while missiles and autocannon fire cross the battlefield.

Make movement an important part of combat, not just a way to travel from one location to another.

The game should be much faster, more fluid, more exciting, and visually impressive while preserving the mechanical identity of the original mechs.